      "aiReviewer": "/root/chemie_native9_fresh_independent_a_20261016",
      "aiNotes": "Own independent V-only review f03-exact-native-png-independent-a-20261016-v1. The comparison depicts the same covered solution warm and after cooling: clear warm vessel, cold vessel with solid crystals and a clear liquid phase above. Both liquid levels are comparable and covered vessels plus the final bound alt text explicitly rule out water loss for this depicted case. There is no evaporation arrow or dilution step being confused with cooling. For the expressly schematic example substance with increasing solubility as temperature rises, cooling from the high-temperature to the low-temperature saturation limit can produce crystals when the initial dissolved amount exceeds the cold limit. The rising curve has Temperature on the horizontal axis and Solubility on the vertical; its cooling arrow points from upper right toward lower left. The top warm-to-cold arrow and crystal formation agree with this mechanism. It is not a universal claim for all solids or all arbitrary unsaturated solutions. Thermometer bulbs are immersed; warm red column is visibly higher and cold blue column lower. These are qualitative thermometers without a false quantitative scale. The crystal drawing is a schematic solute solid, not labelled ice or a particular chemical crystal structure. The final resource description and alt text explicitly identify an example curve and no actual measurement data. Unnumbered smooth curve with two schematic endpoints gives no invented measured numeric values. This illustration cannot replace the learner’s own investigation, data graph or observation evidence. The optional image can illustrate the cooling route without also illustrating every solvent-loss or flat-solubility-curve case. Its use remains within the actual whole learning goal; no approval of the still-unread new P design, source-completeness, practical procedure, atomariness or Memory judgment follows. Actual360CSS: At the actually viewed360x203CSS image, warm/kalt/Abkühlen and both graph axes remain readable. Covered same-vessel comparison, higher/lower thermometer columns, crystals only in the cooled vessel, rising curve and down-left cooling arrow are distinguishable without small-text dependence. No cropping or hidden essential condition observed. Actual680CSS: At actual680x383CSS all labels and umlauts, diagram direction, vessel/liquid/crystal separation and qualitative thermometer behaviour remain clear. Native1672x941 supports the same interpretation without an additional hidden fine-print claim. FIRST tmp/chemie-m7-resumed-20261016/F03-image-current-independent-a-v1/FIRST.actual-native-and-two-CSS-image-only.independent-a.frozen.json sha256:b9bae92c88b9bb6447854fd4e52875ff368eb0a025cc24a7157f5dbddece8952. Final whole goal tmp/chemie-m7-resumed-20261016/HE-G9-F03-one-coherent-solubility-crystallisation-investigation-author-candidate-v1/NEW.WHOLE-goal.8b2d351e.ACTUAL-IMAGE-BOUND.AUTHOR.inactive.json sha256:2f0b395dbae6f6265136166cfeab1fd9a1fe67b5986c2e1e1882e6f77be26414. No current peer opinion used. Explicit AI approval only; legacy ChatGPT/human fields do not substitute for this exact-asset AI block. No P/NativeD/source-whole/learner/human acceptance."
